  "Number": 12,
  "Question": "The first three terms of a geometric progression are \\(\\sqrt2,\\) \\(\\sqrt[3]2,\\) \\(\\sqrt[6]2.\\) Find the fourth term.",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\sqrt[7]2\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\sqrt[8]2\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\sqrt[9]2\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\sqrt[10]2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Writing the radicals as powers of \\(2\\) makes their ratios easier to calculate. All three terms are positive, and the consecutive ratios are\n\\[\n\\frac{2^{1/3}}{2^{1/2}}=2^{-1/6},\\qquad\n\\frac{2^{1/6}}{2^{1/3}}=2^{-1/6}.\n\\]\nA geometric progression multiplies by the same ratio at each step. Therefore its fourth term is\n\\[\n2^{1/6}\\cdot2^{-1/6}=2^0=1.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(1\\), choice A.",
